At the risk of sounding like I'm flaming David and Compaq, I don't agree
that
fundamental problems such as the one Steve describes are as rare as we'd all
like.

There is a bug in Tru64 5.1A having to do with POSIX shared memory and
the changes made to the Universal Cache Buffer.  I have a small test program
that demonstrates a realtime application using POSIX threads, shared memory,
and semaphores.  This program runs at 50Hz and does some nonsense "work"
during each of the intervals.  The set of work takes about 6 milliseconds
for each
interval on a DS20E with two CPUS and 2GB of memory.

The bug shows up when the 'update' program is run by the OS to sync the
disks.
At that point, my realtime program running at high priority and with FIFO
scheduling
will take anywhere from 12-50 milliseconds to do the same work it was doing
in 6 milliseconds.  In the case of a realtime application running at 50Hz,
as soon
as the work interval takes more than 20 milliseconds, we've just busted a
frame.

This same source compiled on a Tru64 4.0G machine works just fine.  Now,
a realtime application is certainly not the norm for most Compaq customers,
but I
would think those 50 millisecond hits would be awfully painful to customers
running
webservers or database servers on Tru64 5.x.  If you're using POSIX shared
memory
and Tru64 5.1A UNIX, you should be aware that this problem may hit you
pretty hard.

If anyone is interested in obtaining my test program to see for themselves,
please
email me using tlosness as the portion in front of the at symbol and
aerosysengr
for the company name with com at the end (I do hate SPAM, so I won't post
me email address in an obvious manner).

Please note that the current workaround is to replace POSIX shared memory
with SYSV shared memory.  The test source I have also incorporates this and
can be compiled in that manner by setting an #define.

Tom Losness
Aero Systems Engineering


