While I can understand how someone completely dependent on realtime shared 
memory synchronization might consider this problem "fundamental", and 
certainly that you might be upset, it's hardly on the same level of 
"fundamental" as a condition variable wait failing to wake up. You started 
out, to begin with, by listing a whole set of specific conditions; the 
original poster has not. As I said, I can only presume there are a lot of 
specialized conditions that aren't explained and cannot be inferred from 
the description.

By the way -- if you're writing a realtime thread program that's dependent 
on realtime synchronization with external state (such as hardware, or 
threads in other processes), I hope you're using SCS (system contention 
scope) threads? While the default PCS threads are more system-friendly and 
lighter-weight for most applications, they're NOT appropriate for such 
realtime operations because the scheduling state of a PCS thread contends 
only against other threads within the process. The kernel threads (or 
"virtual processors") on which PCS threads are scheduled have the kernel 
policy and priority of the process, which is generally lower than the 
visible thread state (e.g., the default timeshare priority 19).

