> Since I've removed the original disk and installed the new disk to the
> slot where the original disk used to be, the new disk is recognized by
> system as /dev/disk/dsk0c, I'm not clear what does you mean that it's
> likely called /dev/disk/dsk2c.
The disk names have no relationship to the SCSI ID's under version  5
When you started you had a disk called dsk0 which had the original system
on.
To this you added a disk which became identified as dsk2. This disk had
a unique wwid.
The wwid is causing the device to register itself as dsk2 no matter where
you put it on the scsi bus.
Because of this even if you remove the original dsk0 the new one won't be
dsk0.
HTH
Adam
PS If you want to boot to single usermode on the new disk and then use
hwmgr to swap dsk2 and dsk0 then that should also fix the problem.
I don't have a scratch system to test the exact syntax so I won't report
it here.






