You should really post the version of your OS when you ask questions
in this forum.  I am also assuming you mean root password.  These are
the steps to reset the root password:

1.  You need to hit the halt button to get to the console prompt.  Do
not power it off completely as it will just boot back into Tru64, we
need it to HALT.  Generally, it has a circle with a triangle as it's
marking.  This is like the bios on a pc.  When you are halted you
should see a prompt that looks like:

>>>

2.  Type in the following command exactly to boot to single user mode:

boot -fl s

3.  Once you are in single user mode you whould see:

INIT: SINGLE-USER MODE

Or something similar.  Hit the enter key and you should get a prompt
like this:

hostname>

4.  Type in the following command to mount the local file systems:

/sbin/bchechrc

5.  When that is finished type in the following command:

psswd root

It will prompt you for a password.  You will have to enter it twice.

6. Type in this command:

sync

7. Hit 'ctrl-d', the Ctrl key and the 'D' key at the same time.  This
will boot you up to Tru64 normally.  You should be able to log in as
root with the new password.  


__
