peter@abbnm.com (Peter da Silva) writes:
> Thomas Anders  <anders@hmi.de> wrote:
> > Unfortunately the output of a cron job gets mailed with an empty
> > subject line by default. Is there a way to globally change this
> > behaviour in Tru64 Unix 5.1A without doing a "mailx -s" (or similar)
> > in every single cron job?
> 
> Two solutions:
> 
> 1. Cron sends mail by calling /usr/bin/mail explicitly. Rename that to
>    /usr/bin/mail.real and create a new "/usr/bin/mail" that looks for
>    the line "Cron: The previous message is the standard output" and
>    does whatever it is you want instead.
> 
> 2. Replace cron with one of the Open Source versions, such as this
>    one: http://freshmeat.net/projects/dcron/?topic_id=141 and you
>    can change its behaviour any way you want.

What I ended up doing was similar to 1., but the other way around
to not (potentially) break other programs using /usr/bin/mail:

1. edit /usr/sbin/cron to change the string "/usr/bin/mail" to
   "/foo/bin/mail" (two times; use Emacs hexl-mode or replace)
2. create a shell script /foo/bin/mail like this:

   #/bin/sh
   (echo "Subject: `hostname` cron output"; cat -) | /usr/bin/mail ${1+"$@"}

Well, I was trying to avoid these sort of things ;)

Kind regards,
Thomas

