In article <3CB42BCA.1040908@studenti.unina.it>,
Marco  <marco@studenti.unina.it> wrote:
  
> # /usr/local/apache2/bin/apachectl start
> 186251:/usr/local/apache2/bin/httpd: /sbin/loader: Fatal Error: call to 
> unresolved symbol from /usr/local/apache2/lib/libapr.so (pc=0x300000266d0)
> /usr/local/apache2/bin/apachectl start: httpd could not be started

Your libapr.so shared object requires a symbol that /sbin/loader can't find.
It's probably having problems finding the OpenSSL library, which was likely
built as a shared library as well.  You should be able to run

	ldd /usr/local/apache2/lib/libapr.so

and see which shared objects the loader can't find.

You either need to make sure that when libapr.so is created, the linker is
told of additional directories to search for shared objects, or you need
to build the search path into the underlying shared objects themselves.
I prefer the latter, because it makes it very easy to link with *and use*
libraries that are installed in places out of the standard loader search path.

Assuming that it is the OpenSSL library that the loader can't find, consider
rebuilding your OpenSSL library using the (non-default) config target of
`alpha-cc-rpath'.  I contributed the code to make that target available,
and I know it works because I've been using it for a long time.  Once you
have the OpenSSL library rebuilt, you should be able to run

	odump -D /path/to/lib/libssl.so

and see an

	RPATH: /path/to/lib

entry.  Once you have that, you can rebuild Apache and it should be able to
find OpenSSL from that point on.

Tim
